{{harvnb|Urquhart|2006}}</ref>

During the foundational events of the Nakba in 1948, [[Killings and massacres during the 1948 Palestine war|dozens of massacres targeting Arabs]] were conducted and about [[List of towns and villages depopulated during the 1947–1949 Palestine war|400 Arab-majority towns and villages were depopulated]].{{sfn|Slater|2020|p=406}} Many of these areas were either completely destroyed or repopulated by Jewish residents and [[Hebraization of Palestinian place names|given new Hebrew names]]. Approximately 750,000<ref name="750k2">{{harvnb|Abu-Laban|Bakan|2022|p=511|ps=, "over 80 per cent"}};

{{harvnb|Pappé|2022|p=128|ps=, "Three-quarters of a million Palestinians{{nbsp}}... almost 90 per cent"}};

{{harvnb|Khalidi|2020|p=60|ps=, "Some 80 percent{{nbsp}}... At least 720,000{{nbsp}}..."}};

{{harvnb|Slater|2020|ps=, pp. 81 ("about 750,000"), 83 ("over 80 percent"), and 350 ("It is no longer a matter of serious dispute that in the 1947–48 period—beginning well before the Arab invasion in May 1948—some 700,000 to 750,000 Palestinians were expelled from or fled their villages and homes in Israel in fear of their lives—an entirely justifiable fear, in light of massacres carried out by Zionist forces.")}};

{{harvnb|Shenhav|2019|p=49|ps=, "750,000"}};

{{harvnb|Bashir|Goldberg|2018|p=7|ps=, "some 750,000"}};

{{harvnb|Manna|2013|ps=, pp. 93 ("approximately 750,000") and 99 n. 12 ("Recently, both Palestinian and Israeli scholars seem to agree on this estimate of 700,000–750,000 refugees.")}};

{{harvnb|Masalha|2012|p=2|ps=, "about 90 per cent{{nbsp}}... 750,000 refugees"}};

{{harvnb|Lentin|2010|ps=, pp. 6 ("at least 80 per cent") and 7 ("more than 700,000")}};

{{harvnb|Kimmerling|2008|p=280|ps=, "700,000 to 900,000"}};

{{harvnb|Sa'di|2007|p=297|ps=, "at least 780,000{{nbsp}}... more than 80 percent"}}</ref> Palestinian Arabs (about half of Palestine's Arab population) [[1948 Palestinian expulsion and flight|fled from their homes or were expelled]] by Zionist militias and later the Israeli army.{{sfn|Usher|2006|p=11–12}}

In 2010, historians [[Martin Shaw (sociologist)|Martin Shaw]] and [[Omer Bartov]] debated whether the 1948 [[Nakba]] should be regarded as a genocide, with Shaw arguing that it could and with Bartov disagreeing.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Shaw |first1=Martin |author1-link=Martin Shaw (sociologist) |last2=Bartov |first2=Omer |author2-link=Omer Bartov |date=2010 |title=The question of genocide in Palestine, 1948: an exchange between Martin Shaw and Omer Bartov |journal=[[Journal of Genocide Research]] |volume=12 |issue=3–4 |pages=243–259 |doi=10.1080/14623528.2010.529698 |s2cid=71620701}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Shaw|2010|pp=2–3, 20–21}}; {{harvnb|Shaw|2013|pp=4–5}}; {{harvnb|Shaw|2024|p=2}}</ref> [[Daud Abdullah]], the former Deputy Secretary General of the [[Muslim Council of Britain]], has said: "Given the declared intent of the Zionist leaders, this wholesale destruction and depopulation of Palestinian villages fit[s] easily with the definition of genocide as cited in the [[Genocide Convention|Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide]]."<ref>{{cite book |last1=Abdullah |first1=Daud |author1-link=Daud Abdullah |title=Cultural Genocide: Law, Politics, and Global Manifestations |date=2019 |publisher=[[Routledge]] |isbn=978-1-351-21410-0 |editor-last=Bachman |editor-first=Jeffrey S. |pages=227–245 |chapter=A century of cultural genocide in Palestine |doi=10.4324/9781351214100-10 |chapter-url=https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/oa-edit/10.4324/9781351214100-10/century-cultural-genocide-palestine-daud-abdullah |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231019172058/https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/oa-edit/10.4324/9781351214100-10/century-cultural-genocide-palestine-daud-abdullah |archive-date=19 October 2023 |s2cid=199268671}}</ref>{{sfn|Center for Constitutional Rights|2016|pp=5–6}} Australian historian [[John Docker]] has also argued that the Nakba is a case of genocide.{{sfn|Docker|2012|pp=22–24}}{{sfn|Rashed|Short|Docker|2014|pp=13–14}} Several scholars have written that Palestinians suffered [[ethnic cleansing]] during the Nakba, but that they did not consider the event to have been genocide.{{efn|[[Ilan Pappé]] (2006),{{sfn|Pappé|2006}}{{sfn|Docker|2012|p=3}} [[Mark Levene]] (2007),<ref>{{cite journal |last=Levene |first=Mark |author-link=Mark Levene |date=December 2007 |title=Book Reviews: The Ethnic Cleansing of Palestine |journal=[[Journal of Genocide Research]] |publisher=[[Routledge]] |volume=9 |number=4 |pages=675–681 |doi=10.1080/14623520701644465 |s2cid=216141849}}</ref> [[Ronit Lentin]] (2010),{{sfn|Lentin|2010|pp=20–21, 23}} [[Derek Penslar]] (2013),<ref name="Kalmus">{{cite news |last1=Kalmus |first1=Jonathan |title=Israel studies professor: 1948 really was ethnic cleansing, not genocide |url=https://www.thejc.com/news/israel-studies-professor-1948-really-was-ethnic-cleansing-not-genocide-quxgdeuv |access-date=16 November 2023 |work=[[The Jewish Chronicle]] |date=14 March 2013 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130317061315/https://www.thejc.com/news/uk-news/103408/israel-studies-professor-1948-really-was-ethnic-cleansing-not-genocide |archive-date=17 March 2013}}</ref> [[Yair Auron]] (2017),<ref name="Rebirth">{{cite book |last1=Auron |first1=Yair |author1-link=Yair Auron |title=The Holocaust, Rebirth, and the Nakba: Memory and Contemporary Israeli–Arab Relations |date=4 October 2017 |publisher=[[Lexington Books]] |isbn=978-1-4985-5949-2 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iD1DDwAAQBAJ |access-date=12 November 2023}}</ref> [[Alon Confino]] (2018), Jerome Slater (2020){{sfn|Slater|2020|pp=80–87}}}}

==== Naksa ====
{{Main|Naksa}}

Academic Clare Brandabur includes the [[Naksa]] (known as the 1967 Palestinian exodus) in the actions conducted by Israel that show a pattern that seeks the "destruction of the national pattern of the oppressed group".<ref name="Brandabur2">{{cite journal |last=Brandabur |first=Clare |date=September 2004 |title=Roadmap to genocide |url=https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?id=GALE%7CA186321548&sid=googleScholar&v=2.1&it=r&linkaccess=abs&issn=14497751&p=AONE&sw=w |journal=Nebula |volume=5 |pages=25–49 |number=3 |archive-date=16 October 2023 |access-date=15 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231016003943/https://go.gale.com/ps/i.do?id=GALE%7CA186321548&sid=googleScholar&v=2.1&it=r&linkaccess=abs&issn=14497751&p=AONE&sw=w |url-status=live}}</ref> This view is supported by later academics as well.{{sfn|Ali|2012|pp=78–80}} The Naksa was the expulsion of around 280,000 to 325,000 Palestinians during and in the aftermath of the [[Six-Day War]], including the razing of numerous Palestinian villages.<ref>{{cite book |last=Bowker |first=Robert P. G. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=LOYtfyJFBIQC |title=Palestinian Refugees: Mythology, Identity, and the Search for Peace |date=2003 |publisher=Lynne Rienner Publishers |isbn=1-58826-202-2 |page=81}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Gerson |first=Allan |url=http://www.hjil.org/articles/hjil-2-2-levie.pdf |title=Israel, the West Bank and International Law |date=1978 |publisher=[[Psychology Press]] |isbn=0-7146-3091-8 |page=162 |archive-date=27 March 2023 |access-date=30 January 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230327132351/http://www.hjil.org/articles/hjil-2-2-levie.pdf }}</ref>{{sfn|Usher|2006|pp=12, 14}}

==== Sabra and Shatila massacre ====
{{Main|Sabra and Shatila massacre}}

[[File:SabraShatilaMemorial.jpg|right|thumb|Memorial for the dead killed in the massacre in [[Sabra and Shatila massacre|Sabra, South Beirut]]]]
In September 1982, between 460 and 3,500 civilians{{Emdash}}mostly [[Palestinians in Lebanon|Palestinians]] and [[Lebanese Shia Muslims]]{{Emdash}}were killed in [[Beirut]]'s Sabra neighborhood and in the adjacent [[Shatila refugee camp]] during the [[Lebanese Civil War]].<ref>{{harvnb|Schiff|Ya'ari|1985|p=[https://archive.org/details/israelslebanonwa00zeev/page/282 282]}}; {{harvnb|Nuwayhed al-Hout|2004|p=296}}; {{harvnb|Fisk|2002}}; {{harvnb|Kapeliouk|1982|p=}}</ref> The killings were carried out by the [[Lebanese Forces (militia)|Lebanese Forces]], one of the main [[Lebanese Front|Christian militias in Lebanon]] at the time. Between the evening of 16 September and the morning of 18 September, the Lebanese militia carried out the killings while the [[Israel Defense Forces]] (IDF) had the Palestinian camp surrounded.<ref>{{harvnb|Fisk|2001|pp=[https://books.google.com/books?id=VrXpeELOUNsC&pg=PA374 382–383]}}; {{harvnb|Quandt|2001|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=-rmCPnSghbcC&pg=PA256 266]}}; {{harvnb|Alpher|2015|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=eCxyBgAAQBAJ&pg=PA48 48]}}; {{harvnb|Gonzalez|2013|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=HypnAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA113 113]}}</ref> The IDF had ordered the militia to clear out the fighters of the [[Palestine Liberation Organization]] (PLO) from Sabra and Shatila as part of a larger Israeli maneuver into western Beirut. As the massacre unfolded, the IDF received reports of atrocities being committed, but did not take any action to stop it.<ref name="malone2">{{cite journal |last=Malone |first=Linda A. |year=1985 |title=The Kahan Report, Ariel Sharon and the Sabra-Shatilla Massacres in Lebanon: Responsibility Under International Law for Massacres of Civilian Populations |url=http://scholarship.law.wm.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1606&context=facpubs&sei-redir=1&referer=http%3A%2F%2Fscholar.google.com.tr%2Fscholar%3Fhl%3Den%26q%3Dmassacres%2Bin%2Blebanon%26btnG%3D%26as_sdt%3D1%252C5%26as_sdtp%3D#search=%22massacres%20lebanon%22 |journal=[[Utah Law Review]] |pages=373–433 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230417035542/https://scholarship.law.wm.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?referer=&httpsredir=1&article=1606&context=facpubs&sei-redir=1&referer=http%3A%2F%2Fscholar.google.com.tr%2Fscholar%3Fhl%3Den&q=massacres+in+lebanon&btnG=&as_sdt=1%2C5&as_sdtp=#search=%22massacres%20lebanon%22 |archive-date=17 April 2023 |access-date=1 January 2013 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{sfn|Nijim|2020|pp=26–27}}

On 16 December 1982, the [[United Nations General Assembly]] condemned the Sabra and Shatila massacre and declared it to be an act of genocide.<ref>{{cite web |title=U.N. General Assembly, Resolution 37/123, adopted between 16 and 20 December 1982 |url=https://www.hlrn.org/img/documents/A_RES_37_123_EN.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240226110946/https://www.hlrn.org/img/documents/A_RES_37_123_EN.pdf |archive-date=26 February 2024 |access-date=27 February 2024 |publisher=[[United Nations]]}}</ref>{{sfn|Rashed|Short|2012|pp=1154–1155}}{{sfn|Ali|2012|p=16}} The voting record<ref>{{cite web |title=Voting Summary U.N. General Assembly Resolution 37/123D |url=http://unbisnet.un.org:8080/ipac20/ipac.jsp?profile=voting&index=.VM&term=ares37123d#focus |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110604072753/http://unbisnet.un.org:8080/ipac20/ipac.jsp?profile=voting&index=.VM&term=ares37123d#focus |archive-date=4 June 2011 |access-date=4 January 2010 |publisher=[[United Nations]]}}</ref><ref name="LK-372">{{cite book |last=Kuper |first=Leo |author-link=Leo Kuper |title=Genocide: Conceptual and Historical Dimensions |date=1997 |publisher=[[University of Pennsylvania Press]] |isbn=0-8122-1616-4 |editor-last=Andreopoulos |editor-first=George J. |page=37 |chapter=Theoretical Issues Relating to Genocide: Uses and Abuses}}</ref>{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=455}} on section D of Resolution 37/123 was: yes: 123; no: 0; abstentions: 22; non-voting: 12. The delegate for [[Canada]] stated: "The term genocide cannot, in our view, be applied to this particular inhuman act".{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=455}} The delegate of [[Singapore]] – voting 'yes' – added: "My delegation regrets the use of the term 'an act of genocide'&nbsp;... [as] the term 'genocide' is used to mean acts committed with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnic, racial or religious group." Canada and Singapore questioned whether the General Assembly was competent to determine whether such an event would constitute genocide.{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=455}} The [[Soviet Union]], by contrast, asserted that: "The word for what Israel is doing on Lebanese soil is genocide. Its purpose is to destroy the Palestinians as a nation."{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=454}} The [[Nicaragua]] delegate asserted: "It is difficult to believe that a [[The Holocaust|people that suffered so much from the Nazi policy of extermination in the middle of the twentieth century]] would use the same fascist, genocidal arguments and methods against other peoples."{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=454}} The United States commented that "While the criminality of the massacre was beyond question, it was a serious and reckless misuse of language to label this tragedy genocide as defined in the 1948 Convention".{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=455}} [[William Schabas]], director of the Irish Centre for Human Rights at the [[National University of Ireland]],<ref>{{cite web |title=Professor William A. Schabas |url=http://www.nuigalway.ie/human_rights/Staff/william_schabas.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070609152022/http://www.nuigalway.ie/human_rights/Staff/william_schabas.html |archive-date=9 June 2007 |website=Irish Centre for Human Rights at the [[National University of Ireland]]}}</ref> stated: "the term genocide&nbsp;... had obviously been chosen to embarrass Israel rather than out of any concern with legal precision".{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=455}}

[[File:Martyr's square shatila.jpg|right|thumb|Martyr's Square is a site in Shatila refugee camp where one of the mass executions took place during the Sabra and Shatila massacre, as well as burial grounds for some of the victims.]]
That same year an independent commission headed by [[Seán MacBride]] investigated reported violations of International Law by Israel and four of its six members concluded that "the deliberate destruction of the national and cultural rights and identity of the Palestinian people amount[ed] to genocide".{{sfn|Schabas|2000|p=235}}{{sfn|Said|1984|p=27}} In its conclusion, the commission recommended "that a competent international body be designed or established to clarify the conception of genocide in relation to Israeli policies and practices toward the Palestinian people".<ref name="MacBride_19832">{{cite journal |date=1983 |title=Israel in Lebanon: Report of the International Commission to Enquire into Reported Violations of International Law by Israel during Its Invasion of the Lebanon |journal=[[Journal of Palestine Studies]] |volume=12 |issue=3 |pages=117–133 |doi=10.2307/2536156 |jstor=2536156}}</ref> [[David Hirst (journalist)|David Hirst]] believes that while the decision of the U.N. General Assembly could still be called biased, it was harder to say the same about the McBride Commission, as well as about individuals around the world, especially Jews, who shared the opinion of its four members.<ref name="sabrageno2">{{cite book |last=Hirst |first=David |author-link=David Hirst (journalist) |title=Beware of small states: Lebanon, Battleground of the Middle East |publisher=Nation Books |year=2010 |isbn=978-0-571-23741-8 |page=153}}</ref>

The massacre was also investigated by the Israeli [[Kahan Commission]]. The commission concluded that although no Israelis were directly involved in the killings, a number of Israeli government ministers and military were indirectly responsible. They should have taken into account the sentiments of their Lebanese allies after their leader [[Bachir Gemayel]] had been assassinated along with 26 other Phalangists in a bomb attack 2 days earlier,{{sfn|Schiff|Ya'ari|1985|pp=246, 265}} and also have taken decisive action to stop the killings when the first information was received.<ref>{{cite web |title=104 Report of the Commission of Inquiry into the events at the refugee camps in Beirut- 8 February 1983 |url=https://www.mfa.gov.il/mfa/foreignpolicy/mfadocuments/yearbook6/pages/104%20report%20of%20the%20commission%20of%20inquiry%20into%20the%20e.aspx |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150720132725/https://www.mfa.gov.il/mfa/foreignpolicy/mfadocuments/yearbook6/pages/104%20report%20of%20the%20commission%20of%20inquiry%20into%20the%20e.aspx |archive-date=20 July 2015 |website=[[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Israel)|Israel Ministry of Foreign Affairs]]}}</ref> The commission's findings were reluctantly accepted by the Israeli government, amid violent, rival, pro- and anti-government protests.<ref name="Eric Silver_19842">{{cite book |last=Silver |first=Eric |url=https://archive.org/details/beginhauntedprop00silv/page/239 |title=Begin: The Haunted Prophet |publisher=[[Random House]] |year=1984 |isbn=978-0-394-52826-7 |page=[https://archive.org/details/beginhauntedprop00silv/page/239 239]}}</ref>

In interviews with film director [[Lokman Slim]] in 2005, some of the fighters of the [[Lebanese Front|Lebanese Christian militia]]s reported that, prior to the massacre, the IDF took them to training camps in Israel and showed them documentaries about the Holocaust.<ref name="training with history2">{{cite news |last1=Rmeileh |first1=Rami |date=18 September 2023 |title=Sabra & Shatila echoes past & ongoing Palestinian suffering |url=https://www.newarab.com/opinion/sabra-shatila-echoes-past-ongoing-palestinian-suffering |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240606060114/https://www.newarab.com/opinion/sabra-shatila-echoes-past-ongoing-palestinian-suffering |archive-date=6 June 2024 |access-date=6 June 2024 |work=[[The New Arab]] |language=en}}</ref> The Israelis told the Lebanese fighters that the same would happen to them too, as a minority in Lebanon, if the fighters did not take action against the Palestinians.<ref name="training with history2"/> The film was called ''"Massaker"'', it featured six perpetrators of the Sabra and Shatila massacre, and it was awarded the ''Fipresci Prize'' at the 2005 Berlinale.<ref name="Massaker2">{{cite news |date=6 February 2021 |title=Lokman Slim: The daring Lebanese activist, admired intellectual |url=https://www.newarab.com/news/lokman-slim-daring-lebanese-activist-admired-intellectual |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230921030432/https://www.newarab.com/news/lokman-slim-daring-lebanese-activist-admired-intellectual |archive-date=21 September 2023 |access-date=6 June 2024 |work=[[The New Arab]] |language=en |quote=Their film "Massaker" — which studied six perpetrators of the 1982 Christian militia massacres of 1,000 people at the Sabra and Shatila Palestinian camps in Beirut — was awarded the Fipresci Prize at the 2005 Berlinale.}}</ref>

=== 21st century ===

==== Second Intifada ====
{{main|Second Intifada}}
At the UN-backed [[World Conference against Racism 2001|2001 Durban Conference Against Racism]], the majority of delegates approved a declaration that accused Israel of being a "racist [[apartheid]] state" guilty of "war crimes, acts of genocide and ethnic cleansing". [[Reed Brody]], the then-executive director of [[Human Rights Watch]], criticised the declaration, arguing that "Israel has committed serious crimes against Palestinian people but it is simply not accurate to use the word genocide", while Claudio Cordone, a spokesman for [[Amnesty International]], stated that "[w]e are not ready to make the assertion that Israel is engaged in genocide".<ref>{{Cite news |title=Israel branded 'racist' by rights forum |date=2 September 2001 |url=http://edition.cnn.com/2001/WORLD/africa/09/02/durban.racism/ |work=[[CNN]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240520043739/http://edition.cnn.com/2001/WORLD/africa/09/02/durban.racism/ |archive-date=20 May 2024 |access-date=24 August 2025}}</ref>

During the 2002 [[Battle of Jenin (2002)|battle of Jenin]], numerous Palestinian officials,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lee |first=Ken |date=24 June 2003 |title=Jenin rises from the dirt |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/3015814.stm |work=[[BBC News]] |access-date=24 August 2025 |language=en-GB |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250703030308/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/3015814.stm |archive-date=3 July 2025}}</ref> as well as a British forensics expert who entered the camp as part of an [[Amnesty International]] team, accused the [[Israel Defense Forces]] of carrying out a massacre of Palestinian civilians numbering in the 100s at the [[Jenin refugee camp]] in the [[West Bank]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 April 2002 |title=Jenin 'massacre evidence growing' |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/1937048.stm |work=[[BBC News]] |access-date=24 August 2025 |language=en-GB |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250112120043/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/1937048.stm |archive-date=12 January 2025}}</ref> [[A. N. Wilson]], a columnist for the British newspaper ''[[The Independent]]'', wrote that "we are talking here of massacre, and a cover-up, of genocide",<ref>{{cite news |date=8 August 2002 |title=Naught for your comfort |url=https://www.economist.com/leaders/2002/08/08/naught-for-your-comfort |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220723013321/https://www.economist.com/leaders/2002/08/08/naught-for-your-comfort |archive-date=23 July 2022}}</ref> while [[Yasmin Alibhai-Brown]] called for [[Ariel Sharon]] to be tried for crimes against humanity.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Alibhai-Brown |first=Yasmin |author-link=Yasmin Alibhai-Brown |date=17 April 2002 |title=Why I'm Boycotting Anything 'Made in Israel' |url=https://www.converge.org.nz/pma/cra0399.htm |work=[[The Independent]] |via=converge.org.nz |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250222013607/https://www.converge.org.nz/pma/cra0399.htm |archive-date=22 February 2025}}</ref> Subsequent investigations by Human Rights Watch and the [[United Nations]] confirmed that there had been no massacre, but accused Israel of committing war crimes.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Wood |first=Paul |date=3 May 2002 |title='No Jenin massacre' says rights group |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/1965471.stm |work=[[BBC News]] |access-date=24 August 2025 |language=en-GB |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120710205712/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/1965471.stm |archive-date=10 July 2012}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Rees |first=Matt |date=13 May 2002 |title=Untangling Jenin's Tale |url=http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,1002406-1,00.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121023201722/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,1002406-1,00.html |archive-date=23 October 2012 |access-date=24 August 2025 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |language=en-us}}</ref>

==== Blockade of Gaza ====
{{Main|Blockade of the Gaza Strip}}

[[File:Israeli blockade of gaza by latuff2-375x239.jpg|thumb|Cartoon by [[Carlos Latuff]] depicting the consequences of Israel's blockade on Gaza in 2010<ref>{{cite web |author=Global Voices |date=28 May 2010 |title=Israel: The Freedom Flotilla - PR Stunt or Humanitarian Act? |url=http://www.alertnet.org/db/blogs/29542/4e134cda2e86b825d4534e2f0277cf16.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100709030411/http://www.alertnet.org/db/blogs/29542/4e134cda2e86b825d4534e2f0277cf16.htm |archive-date=9 July 2010 |website=[[Reuters]] AlertNet}}</ref>]]
The restrictions on movement and goods in Gaza imposed by [[Israel]] date to the early 1990s.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 August 2012 |title=Light at the End of Their Tunnels? Hamas and the Arab Uprisings |url=https://www.crisisgroup.org/~/media/Files/Middle%20East%20North%20Africa/Israel%20Palestine/129-light-at-the-end-of-their-tunnels-hamas-and-the-arab-uprisings.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120902185323/https://www.crisisgroup.org/~/media/Files/Middle%20East%20North%20Africa/Israel%20Palestine/129-light-at-the-end-of-their-tunnels-hamas-and-the-arab-uprisings.pdf |archive-date=2 September 2012 |website=[[International Crisis Group]] |publisher= |page=38, note 283 |quote=The prime minister is comfortable with limited economic growth in Gaza, particularly as a way to modify Hamas's urge to get into trouble. We still want there to be a discrepancy between economic life in Gaza and the West Bank, but we no longer feel it needs to be so large. |access-date=1 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> After [[Hamas]] took over in 2007, Israel significantly intensified existing movement restrictions and imposed a complete [[blockade]] on the movement of goods and people in and out of the [[Gaza Strip]].<ref>{{cite web |date=28 June 2023 |title=Gaza Up Close |url=https://features.gisha.org/gaza-up-close |website=Gisha – Legal Center for Freedom of Movement |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230829134339/https://features.gisha.org/gaza-up-close/ |archive-date=29 August 2023}}</ref> In the same year, Egypt closed the [[Rafah Border Crossing]].<ref>{{Cite news|agency=Associated Press|date=25 May 2011 |title=Egypt to reopen Gaza strip crossing for first time since 2007 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2011/may/25/egypt-to-reopen-gaza-strip-crossing |access-date=1 March 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241227202652/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2011/may/25/egypt-to-reopen-gaza-strip-crossing |archive-date=27 December 2024}}</ref> Many protestors across the globe called the blockade an act of genocide,<ref>{{cite news |date=28 December 2008 |title=World stands united against 'genocide' in Gaza |url=https://english.alarabiya.net/articles/2008%2F12%2F28%2F62977 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211129183345/https://english.alarabiya.net/articles/2008/12/28/62977 |archive-date=29 November 2021 |work=[[Al Arabiya]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=5 January 2009 |title=Australian demonstrations show solidarity with Palestinian people |url=https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2009/01/aust-j05.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190602063856/https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2009/01/aust-j05.html |archive-date=2 June 2019 |work=[[World Socialist Web Site]]}}</ref> with the Venezuelan president [[Hugo Chavez]] recalling Venezuela's ambassador in Israel and calling Israel's attacks "genocide".<ref>{{cite news |date=7 January 2009 |title=Caracas expels Israeli ambassador |url=https://www.france24.com/en/20090107-caracas-expels-israeli-ambassador- |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618235637/https://www.france24.com/en/20090107-caracas-expels-israeli-ambassador- |archive-date=18 June 2021 |work=[[France 24]] |agency=[[Agence France-Press]]}}</ref>

Israeli [[New Historians|New Historian]] [[Ilan Pappé]] has argued that genocide "is the only appropriate way to describe what the Israeli army is doing in the Gaza Strip".{{sfn|Lendman|2010|p=30}}{{sfn|Pappé|2010|pp=201–205}} In his 2017 book, ''Ten Myths About Israel'', Pappé wrote: "Israel's claim that its actions since 2006 have been part of a self-defensive war against terror. I will venture to call{{nbsp}}... an incremental genocide of the people of Gaza."<ref>{{cite book |last1=Pappé |first1=Ilan |title=Ten Myths About Israel |date=2017 |publisher=[[Verso Books]] |isbn=978-1-78663-019-3 |page=262}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Dvais |first=Charles |date=13 August 2014 |title=Israel's War on Palestine: It's Bad, but Is It 'Genocide'? |url=https://www.vice.com/en/article/israels-war-on-gaza-is-it-genocide-813/ |work=[[Vice News]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240815182544/https://www.vice.com/en/article/israels-war-on-gaza-is-it-genocide-813/ |archive-date=15 August 2024}}</ref> In an article published in 2022 in the ''International Journal of Human Rights'', Mohammed Nijim voiced his belief "that Israeli policies that were enacted after the introduction of the [[Blockade of the Gaza Strip]] amount[ed] to slow-motion genocide",{{sfn|Nijim|2023|pp=165, 173, 176–177}} following previous descriptions of Israel's treatment of Palestinians as a "slow-motion genocide".{{sfn|Cook|2010|p=24}}{{sfn|Lendman|2010|pp=29–38}} This is in line with [[Uğur Ümit Üngör]]'s application of Lemkin's description of genocide as the "imposition of the national pattern" as being "genocidal as incremental forms of slow violence."{{sfn|Üngör|2024|pp=5–6}} Through the period of the blockade various other commentators from political and news organisations have labelled the blockade variously as genocide and sociocide.<ref>{{cite web |last=Al-Jaza'iri |first=Isa |date=2 June 2010 |title=Flotilla massacre exposes criminal blockade of Gaza |url=https://www.marxist.com/flotilla-massacre-exposes-blockade-of-gaza.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240118234812/https://www.marxist.com/flotilla-massacre-exposes-blockade-of-gaza.htm |archive-date=18 January 2024 |website=[[International Marxist Tendency|In Defence of Marxism]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Feldman |first=Allen |date=12 August 2014 |title=Genocidal desistance in Gaza |url=https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/north-africa-west-asia/genocidal-desistance-in-gaza-0/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200812101932/https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/north-africa-west-asia/genocidal-desistance-in-gaza-0/ |archive-date=12 August 2020 |work=[[openDemocracy]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Armstrong |first=Mick |date=6 August 2014 |title=What is happening in Palestine is genocide |url=https://redflag.org.au/article/what-happening-palestine-genocide |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210511051636/https://redflag.org.au/article/what-happening-palestine-genocide |archive-date=11 May 2021 |website=Red Flag |publisher=[[Socialist Alternative (Australia)|Socialist Alternative]]}}</ref> The 2007 blockade has continued since its implementation.<ref>{{cite news |date=14 March 2021 |title=Gaza Strip: A beginner's guide to an enclave under blockade |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/3/14/a-guide-to-the-gaza-strip |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210701025747/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/3/14/a-guide-to-the-gaza-strip |archive-date=1 July 2021 |work=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=30 June 2022 |title=Gaza Strip: The humanitarian impact of 15 years of the blockade - June 2022 |url=https://www.ochaopt.org/content/gaza-strip-humanitarian-impact-15-years-blockade-june-2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610020226/https://ochaopt.org/content/gaza-strip-humanitarian-impact-15-years-blockade-june-2022 |archive-date=10 June 2023 |website=[[OCHA]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=14 June 2022 |title=Gaza: Israel's 'Open-Air Prison' at 15 |url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/06/14/gaza-israels-open-air-prison-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231001105927/https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/06/14/gaza-israels-open-air-prison-15 |archive-date=1 October 2023 |access-date=12 October 2023 |work=[[Human Rights Watch]]}}</ref>

==== 2008 Gaza War ====
{{Main|Gaza War (2008–2009)}}

{{see also|2008–2009 Gaza Strip aid}}
[[File:A Palestinian baby killed by the IDF.jpg|thumb|The charred body of a Palestinian baby killed in an Israeli strike then run over by a tank during the [[Gaza War (2008–2009)|2008–2009 Israel-Gaza conflict]]]]
The 2008 Gaza War, also known as 'Operation Cast Lead'<ref>{{cite book |last=Gavriely-Nuri |first=Dalia |title=The Normalization of War in Israeli Discourse, 1967–2008 |date=2013 |publisher=[[Lexington Books]] |isbn=978-0-7391-7260-5 |location=Lanham, MD |pages=42–43}}</ref> and the 'Gaza Massacre',<ref>{{cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bQviYWQZGCUC&pg=PA1 |title=Gaza: Morality, Law & Politics |publisher=UWA Publishing |year=2010 |isbn=978-1-74258-096-8 |editor-last=Gaita |editor-first=Raimond |editor-link=Raimond Gaita |page=1}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Fisher |first1=David |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Rxtr6qSB2zcC&pg=PA164 |title=Just war on terror?: A Christian and Muslim response |last2=Wicker |first2=Brian |publisher=[[Ashgate Publishing]] |year=2010 |isbn=978-1-4094-0808-6 |page=164}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Wiegand |first=Krista |author1-link=Krista E. Wiegand |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=WlDkiLkscSQC&pg=PA131 |title=Bombs and ballots: governance by Islamist terrorist and guerrilla groups |publisher=[[Ashgate Publishing]] |year=2010 |isbn=978-0-7546-7891-5 |page=131}}</ref> was a three-week armed conflict between Gaza Strip Palestinian paramilitary groups and the Israel Defense Forces that [[Timeline of the 2008–2009 Israel–Gaza conflict#27 December 2008|began]] on 27 December 2008 and ended on 18 January 2009 with a unilateral ceasefire. The conflict resulted in 1,166–1,417 Palestinian and 13 Israeli deaths.<ref>{{cite news |last=Al-Mughrabi |first=Nidal |title=Israel tightens grip on urban parts of Gaza |url=http://www.newsdaily.com/stories/tre5053r7-us-palestinians-israel/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090109194300/http://www.newsdaily.com/stories/tre5053r7-us-palestinians-israel/ |archive-date=9 January 2009 |work=News Daily}}</ref> Over 46,000 homes were destroyed in Gaza, making more than 100,000 people homeless.<ref>{{cite book |last=Filiu |first=Jean-Pierre |author-link=Jean-Pierre Filiu |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4fhzBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA207 |title=Gaza: A History |date=2014 |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |isbn=978-0-19-020189-0 |page=318}}</ref>

A [[2008 Israel–Hamas ceasefire|six month long ceasefire between Israel and Hamas]] ended on 4 November, when the IDF made a raid into [[Deir al-Balah]], central Gaza to destroy a tunnel, killing several Hamas militants.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Richemond-Barak |first=Daphné |date=2018 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZBBADwAAQBAJ&pg=PA27 |title=Underground Warfare |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |page=27 |isbn=978-0-19-045724-2 |language=en}}</ref> Israel said the raid was a preemptive strike and Hamas intended to abduct further Israeli soldiers,<ref name="CRS2009"/><ref name="truce broken">{{Cite news |last=McCarthy |first=Rory |date=5 November 2008 |title=Gaza truce broken as Israeli raid kills six Hamas gunmen |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2008/nov/05/israelandthepalestinians |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=26 April 2020 |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250204101455/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2008/nov/05/israelandthepalestinians |archive-date=4 February 2025}}</ref> while Hamas characterized it as a ceasefire violation,<ref name="CRS2009">{{cite report |last1=Zanotti |first1=Jim |last2=Migdalovitz |first2=Carol |last3=Sharp |first3=Jeremy M. |last4=Addis |first4=Casey L. |last5=Blanchard |first5=Christopher M. |last6=Margesson |first6=Rhoda |date=19 February 2009 |url=https://fas.org/sgp/crs/mideast/R40101.pdf |pages=6–7 |title=Israel and Hamas: Conflict in Gaza (2008–2009) |publisher=[[Congressional Research Service]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250114123650/https://sgp.fas.org/crs/mideast/R40101.pdf |archive-date=14 January 2025}}</ref><ref name="truce broken"/> and responded with [[Palestinian rocket attacks on Israel|rocket fire into Israel]].<ref>{{cite news |date=18 January 2009 |title=Q&A: Gaza conflict |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/7818022.stm |work=[[BBC News]] |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250217152206/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/7818022.stm |archive-date=17 February 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first1=John |last1=Kroll |author2=Torchia |first3=Alfred |last3=de Montesquiou |date=18 January 2009 |title=Israeli troops exiting Gaza; Hamas ceases fire |newspaper=The Plain Dealer |location=Cleveland |url=http://www.cleveland.com/world/index.ssf/2009/01/israel_withdraws_from_gaza_str.html |agency=[[Associated Press]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241201180256/https://www.cleveland.com/world/2009/01/israel_withdraws_from_gaza_str.html |archive-date=1 December 2024}}</ref> Attempts to renew a truce between Israel and Hamas were unsuccessful. On 27 December, Israel began Operation Cast Lead with the stated aim of stopping rocket fire.<ref name="guardian.co.uk">{{Cite news |first=Rory |last=McCarthy |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2009/sep/16/israel-rejects-war-crimes-gaza |title=Israel rejects war crimes findings of UN Gaza inquiry |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |date=16 September 2009 |access-date=8 May 2010 |location=London |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250106210714/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2009/sep/16/israel-rejects-war-crimes-gaza |archive-date=6 January 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Bright |first=Arthur |url=https://www.csmonitor.com/2008/1226/p99s01-duts.html |title=Israel set to launch 'limited operation' in Gaza |work=[[The Christian Science Monitor]] |date=26 December 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090308113739/http://www.csmonitor.com/2008/1226/p99s01-duts.html |archive-date=8 March 2009}}</ref>

The United Nations [[Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs]] reported that there was "massive destruction of livelihoods and a significant deterioration of infrastructure and basic services", with 80% of the population no longer being able to support themselves and were dependent on humanitarian assistance.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Gaza Humanitarian Situation Report – January 2, 2009 as of 14:30 |date=2 January 2009 |publisher=[[United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs]] |url=http://www.ochaopt.org/documents/ocha_opt_gaza_situation_report_2009_01_02_english.pdf |access-date=2 January 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090114195338/http://www.ochaopt.org/documents/ocha_opt_gaza_situation_report_2009_01_02_english.pdf |archive-date=14 January 2009}}</ref> The [[International Committee of the Red Cross|International Red Cross]] said the situation was "intolerable" and a "full blown humanitarian crisis".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/7813671.stm |title=Gaza clashes spark 'major crisis' |work=[[BBC News]] |date=6 January 2009 |access-date=2 April 2010 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241208205246/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/middle_east/7813671.stm |archive-date=8 December 2024}}</ref> The [[United Nations Fact Finding Mission on the Gaza Conflict]] found that Israel, at least in part, targeted the people of Gaza as a whole.<ref>{{Cite report |title=Human Rights in Palestine and Other Occupied Arab Territories |date=25 September 2009 |url=http://www2.ohchr.org/english/bodies/hrcouncil/docs/12session/A-HRC-12-48.pdf |publisher=[[United Nations]] |page=paragr. 1680–81 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250220085918/https://www2.ohchr.org/english/bodies/hrcouncil/docs/12session/A-HRC-12-48.pdf |archive-date=20 February 2025}}</ref>

American human rights lawyer [[Francis Boyle]] and historian Ilan Pappé both consider Israel's actions against Gaza in the 2008 Gaza War to be genocidal.<ref>{{cite web |last=Boyle |first=Francis A. |author-link=Francis Boyle |date=28 December 2009 |title=International Law And Israel's War On Gaza |url=https://www.countercurrents.org/boyle281209.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240118234838/https://www.countercurrents.org/boyle281209.htm |archive-date=18 January 2024 |website=[[Countercurrents.org]]}}</ref>

==== 2014 Gaza War ====
{{Main|2014 Gaza War}}

[[File:20140805 beit hanun8.jpg|thumb|Ruins of buildings in [[Beit Hanoun]], August 2014]]
The 2014 Gaza War, also referred to as Operation Protective Edge, was a military operation launched by Israel on 8 July 2014 in the Gaza Strip.{{sfn|Center for Constitutional Rights|2016|p=6}}<ref>{{cite news |last=Zonszein |first=Mairav |date=27 March 2015 |title=Israel killed more Palestinians in 2014 than in any other year since 1967 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/mar/27/israel-kills-more-palestinians-2014-than-any-other-year-since-1967 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200312025011/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/mar/27/israel-kills-more-palestinians-2014-than-any-other-year-since-1967 |archive-date=12 March 2020 |access-date=3 March 2020 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> [[Al-Haq]], a Palestinian Human Rights organization, concluded in a report that serious violations of international law were committed in the course of the 2014 Israeli offensive against Gaza. The organization, along with other Palestinian human rights organizations the [[Palestinian Centre for Human Rights]], [[Al Mezan Center for Human Rights]] and [[Addameer]], submitted a legal file to the [[International Criminal Court]] encouraging it to begin an investigation and prosecution into the crimes against humanity and war crimes committed during the course of Israel's 2014 Gaza offensive. The crime of genocide was referenced as an Israeli crime by these groups.{{sfn|Center for Constitutional Rights|2016|pp=6–7}} Additionally, dozens of [[The Holocaust|Holocaust]] [[Holocaust survivors|survivors]], along with hundreds of descendants of Holocaust survivors and victims, accused Israel of "genocide" for the deaths of more than 2,000 Palestinians in Gaza during the 2014 Gaza War.{{sfn|Center for Constitutional Rights|2016|pp=6–7}}<ref>{{cite news |date=23 August 2014 |title=Holocaust Survivors Condemn Israel for 'Gaza Massacre,' Call for Boycott |url=https://www.haaretz.com/2014-08-23/ty-article/holocaust-survivors-condemn-israel-for-gaza-massacre/0000017f-e738-dea7-adff-f7fb2fbe0000 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220904111631/https://www.haaretz.com/2014-08-23/ty-article/holocaust-survivors-condemn-israel-for-gaza-massacre/0000017f-e738-dea7-adff-f7fb2fbe0000 |archive-date=4 September 2022 |work=[[Haaretz]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Zachary Davies |last=Boren |date=25 August 2014 |title=Holocaust survivors and their descendants accuse Israel of 'genocide' |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/holocaust-survivors-and-their-descendants-accuse-israel-of-genocide-9687994.html |work=[[The Independent]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240708051820/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/holocaust-survivors-and-their-descendants-accuse-israel-of-genocide-9687994.html |archive-date=8 July 2024}}</ref> In a September statement to the United Nations, Palestinian president [[Mahmoud Abbas]] stated that the war amounted to a genocidal crime.<ref>{{cite news |date=27 September 2014 |title=Abbas: Israel waging war of genocide in Gaza |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2014/9/27/abbas-israel-waging-war-of-genocide-in-gaza |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230606061855/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2014/9/27/abbas-israel-waging-war-of-genocide-in-gaza |archive-date=6 June 2023 |work=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]}}</ref> Political analyst and diplomat [[Abukar Arman]] drew parallels between what he called the "genocide in Palestine" and the [[Darfur genocide (2003–2005)|Darfur genocide]], highlighting what he believes to be political motives for the international community labelling Darfur a genocide, but not Palestine.<ref>{{cite news |last=Arman |first=Abukar |author-link=Abukar Arman |date=27 July 2014 |title=On Gaza, genocide, and impunity |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2014/7/27/on-gaza-genocide-and-impunity |work=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240314003210/https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2014/7/27/on-gaza-genocide-and-impunity/ |archive-date=14 March 2024}}</ref>

==== 2021 Israel–Palestine crisis ====
{{Main|2021 Israel–Palestine crisis}}

During the [[2021 Israel–Palestine crisis]], a video circulated on social media showing Israelis celebrating at the [[Western Wall]], whilst a tree near the [[Al-Aqsa Mosque]] burns in the background. A large crowd of Israeli Jews gathered around a fire near the mosque on 10 May, chanting ''[[Yimakh shemo|yimakh shemam]]'', a Hebrew [[curse]] meaning "may their names be erased". [[IfNotNow]] co-founder and [[B'Tselem|B'Tselem USA]] director [[Simone Zimmerman]] criticized them as exhibiting "genocidal animus towards Palestinians — emboldened and unfiltered".<ref>{{cite web |last=Corbett |first=Jessica |date=10 May 2021 |title="These Are Not 'Clashes'": Media Slammed for Coverage Amid Deadly IDF Attacks on Gaza |url=https://www.commondreams.org/news/2021/05/10/these-are-not-clashes-media-slammed-coverage-amid-deadly-idf-attacks-gaza |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210512175812/https://www.commondreams.org/news/2021/05/10/these-are-not-clashes-media-slammed-coverage-amid-deadly-idf-attacks-gaza |archive-date=12 May 2021 |access-date=11 May 2021 |website=[[Common Dreams]]}}</ref><ref name="Mackey 20212">{{cite web |last=Mackey |first=Robert |date=11 May 2021 |title=This Is Not Fine: Why Video of an Ultranationalist Frenzy in Jerusalem Is So Unsettling |url=https://theintercept.com/2021/05/11/not-fine-video-ultranationalist-frenzy-jerusalem-unsettling/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210511091336/https://theintercept.com/2021/05/11/not-fine-video-ultranationalist-frenzy-jerusalem-unsettling/ |archive-date=11 May 2021 |access-date=11 May 2021 |website=[[The Intercept]]}}</ref> ''[[The Intercept]]'' described the video as "unsettling" and an example of "ultranationalist frenzy". [[Ayman Odeh]], a [[Lists of Knesset members|member of the Knesset]] for the [[Joint List]], said the video was "shocking".<ref name="Mackey 20212"/>

In an opinion survey of [[American Jews]], commissioned by the [[Jewish Electorate Institute]] following the 2021 crisis, 22 percent agreed that "Israel is committing genocide against the Palestinians,"{{sfn|JTA|Kampeas|2021}} and Matt Boxer in ''[[The Forward]]'' noted that the poll may have underestimated the percentage of American Jews who have a critical view of Israel because it undercounted secular Jews, who tend to be less attached to Israel.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rosenfeld |first1=Arno |date=15 July 2021 |title=What if a quarter of Jews really do think Israel is a genocidal, apartheid state? |url=https://forward.com/news/473044/what-if-a-quarter-of-jews-really-do-think-israel-is-a-genocidal-apartheid/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231211073410/https://forward.com/news/473044/what-if-a-quarter-of-jews-really-do-think-israel-is-a-genocidal-apartheid/ |archive-date=11 December 2023 |access-date=11 December 2023 |work=[[The Forward]]}}</ref> Conversely, the accusation of genocide during this period was rejected as "ridiculous" and "baseless" by several Jewish and Israeli human rights lawyers, including some who had accused Israel of apartheid.<ref name="Sales, 20212"/>

==== Gaza genocide ====
{{Main|Gaza genocide}}

{{See also|Israeli war crimes#Gaza war (2023–present)|War crimes in the Gaza war#By Israel}}
{{Excerpt|Gaza genocide|hat=no|files=0}}

Israel has been accused of attacking Palestinians using [[collective punishment]],<ref>{{cite web |date=12 October 2023 |title=Israel/OPT: Israel must lift illegal and inhumane blockade on Gaza as power plant runs out of fuel |url=https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2023/10/israel-opt-israel-must-lift-illegal-and-inhumane-blockade-on-gaza-as-power-plant-runs-out-of-fuel/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240508202158/https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2023/10/israel-opt-israel-must-lift-illegal-and-inhumane-blockade-on-gaza-as-power-plant-runs-out-of-fuel/ |archive-date=8 May 2024 |access-date=7 May 2024 |website=[[Amnesty International]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=15 January 2024 |title=UN chief reiterates Gaza ceasefire call, condemns 'collective punishment' of Palestinians |url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2024/01/1145537 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240620113054/https://news.un.org/en/story/2024/01/1145537 |archive-date=20 June 2024 |access-date=7 May 2024 |website=United Nations News |publisher=[[United Nations]]}}</ref> [[forced displacement]] ([[ethnic cleansing]]),<ref>{{bulleted list|
